        ],
        "statement": "Let $(S,m)$ be a finite Coxeter matrix, $W$ the presented group with length $\\ell$, descent sets $D_L,D_R$, weak orders $\\le_R,\\le_L$ and intervals $[u,v]_R,[u,v]_L$ as in [[def-cg-left-right-weak-order-and-descents]], with inversion sets $N(w)=\\{\\alpha\\in\\Phi_+:\\rho(w)\\alpha\\in\\Phi_-\\}$ and the recursion of [[def-cg-geometric-inversion-set]] (2). Then:\n\n**(1) Partial orders.** $\\le_R$ and $\\le_L$ are partial orders on $W$, both with minimum $1$; $u\\le_R v$ and $\\ell(u)=\\ell(v)$ imply $u=v$; and inversion $w\\mapsto w^{-1}$ is an order isomorphism $(W,\\le_R)\\to(W,\\le_L)$, i.e. $u\\le_R v\\iff u^{-1}\\le_L v^{-1}$.\n\n**(2) Covers.** For all $u,v\\in W$,\n$$u\\lessdot_R v\\iff v=us\\text{ for some }s\\in S\\text{ with }\\ell(v)=\\ell(u)+1,\\qquad u\\lessdot_L v\\iff v=su\\text{ for some }s\\in S\\text{ with }\\ell(v)=\\ell(u)+1.$$\nMoreover $u\\le_R v$ if and only if there is a chain $u=u_0\\lessdot_R u_1\\lessdot_R\\cdots\\lessdot_R u_m=v$, and then necessarily $m=\\ell(v)-\\ell(u)$; the same holds in $\\le_L$.\n\n**(3) Intervals are finite and graded.** For every $k\\ge0$ the ball $\\{w\\in W:\\ell(w)\\le k\\}$ is finite, with at most $1+|S|+\\cdots+|S|^k$ elements. Consequently, whenever $u\\le_R v$, the interval $[u,v]_R$ is finite and $x\\mapsto\\ell(x)-\\ell(u)$ is a rank function on it in the sense of [[def-graded-poset-and-rank]]; in particular every maximal chain in $[u,v]_R$ has exactly $\\ell(v)-\\ell(u)+1$ elements, and if $u^{-1}v=s'_1\\cdots s'_m$ is a reduced expression then $u\\lessdot_R us'_1\\lessdot_R\\cdots\\lessdot_R u s'_1\\cdots s'_m=v$ is such a maximal chain. The same statements hold for $\\le_L$.\n\n**(4) Inversion-set criterion.** For all $u,v\\in W$,\n$$u\\le_R v\\iff N(u^{-1})\\subseteq N(v^{-1}),\\qquad u\\le_L v\\iff N(u)\\subseteq N(v);$$\nmoreover $|N(w^{-1})|=|N(w)|=\\ell(w)$ for every $w$. Equivalently, $w\\mapsto N(w^{-1})$ embeds $(W,\\le_R)$ into the lattice of subsets of $\\Phi_+$ as an order-preserving and length-preserving map. The criterion is not the definition of $\\le_R$; it is derived from [[def-cg-left-right-weak-order-and-descents]].\n\n**(5) Descents and roots.** For all $w\\in W$ and $s\\in S$,\n$$s\\in D_L(w)\\iff e_s\\in N(w^{-1})\\iff\\rho(w^{-1})e_s\\in\\Phi_-,\\qquad s\\in D_R(w)\\iff e_s\\in N(w)\\iff\\rho(w)e_s\\in\\Phi_-.$$\nNo Choice is used.",
        "strategy": "(1) Establish reflexivity and the minimum directly from the length-additive definitions, prove transitivity by concatenating length-additive factors, and prove antisymmetry from equal length and the length identity. (2) For covers, expand the quotient in a reduced word; subadditivity and the length of the whole product force every prefix and suffix to be length-additive, so any quotient of length at least two creates an intermediate element. The converse uses the one-unit length interval. Inversion gives the left-handed claim. (3) Count the finitely many words of length at most k; each interval lies in a finite ball, and the length shift is a rank function because interval covers are covers in W. (4) For the forward inversion criterion, extend a reduced prefix and use the prefix-root formula. For the converse, induct on length, choose the first letter, identify the common left descent by the root-length criterion, translate both elements by that descent, and remove the corresponding simple root using the inversion-set recursion. (5) Derive the descent-root equivalences directly from the root-length criterion and inversion-set definition. No Choice is used.",
