        "statement": "Let $(S,m)$ be the Coxeter matrix of type $A_2$: $S=\\{s,t\\}$, $m(s,t)=3$, let $W$ be the presented group with length $\\ell$, identified with the symmetric group $S_3$ by [[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (4), and let $\\le_R,\\le_L$ be the weak orders of [[def-cg-left-right-weak-order-and-descents]]. Then $W=\\{1,s,t,st,ts,w_0\\}$ with $w_0=sts=tst$ and $\\ell(1)=0$, $\\ell(s)=\\ell(t)=1$, $\\ell(st)=\\ell(ts)=2$, $\\ell(w_0)=3$, and:\n\n**(1) Covers and the lattice table.** The right weak order has exactly the cover relations $1\\lessdot_R s$, $1\\lessdot_R t$, $s\\lessdot_R st$, $t\\lessdot_R ts$, $st\\lessdot_R w_0$, $ts\\lessdot_R w_0$, so its Hasse diagram is the hexagon formed by the two chains $1<s<st<w_0$ and $1<t<ts<w_0$. Meets and joins are the following complete table: on elements of one chain, meet and join are the smaller and the larger; across the two chains one has\n$$u\\wedge v=1\\ \\text{ for }\\ (u,v)\\in\\{(s,t),(s,ts),(st,t),(st,ts)\\},\\qquad w_0\\wedge v=v\\ \\text{ for }\\ v\\in\\{s,t,st,ts\\},$$\nand $u\\vee v=w_0$ for every pair $u,v\\notin\\{w_0\\}$ lying in different chains, as well as whenever one of $u,v$ equals $w_0$. In particular $\\bigwedge\\varnothing=w_0$ and $\\bigvee\\varnothing=1$, and every meet and join in the table is the unique element with the corresponding universal bound property.\n\n**(2) Left order and inversion.** The left weak order is the image of the right order under $w\\mapsto w^{-1}$: for example $s\\le_R st$ but $s\\not\\le_L st$, while $t\\le_L st$ but $t\\not\\le_R st$. With the simple roots $\\alpha_s,\\alpha_t$ and the positive root $\\alpha_s+\\alpha_t$ of $A_2$, the six inversion sets $N(w^{-1})$ are\n$$\\varnothing,\\ \\{\\alpha_s\\},\\ \\{\\alpha_t\\},\\ \\{\\alpha_s,\\alpha_s+\\alpha_t\\},\\ \\{\\alpha_t,\\alpha_s+\\alpha_t\\},\\ \\Phi_+$$\nfor $w=1,s,t,st,ts,w_0$ respectively, and the criterion $u\\le_R v\\iff N(u^{-1})\\subseteq N(v^{-1})$ of [